(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609011200-testnet-faucet-and-the-rail-it-feeds")
  (:adr/title "The faucet pours, and the sellers it pours for accept the chain it pours on")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-01")
  (:adr/tags (vector "x402" "faucet" "base-sepolia" "testnet" "nexus-x402" "murakumo" "kotobase"))
  (:adr/body "## Decision

x402.nexus hands out Base Sepolia gas and USDC, and every seller that wants to be
tried on it lists its resources on base-sepolia as well as base. Two halves, and
either alone is useless: tokens that buy nothing, or an offer nobody can pay.

## The pool

Base Sepolia gas cannot be mined -- the chain reports difficulty 0x0 and a
sequencer produces its blocks -- so the only ways in are a captcha-gated web
faucet or a deposit from L1. The workspace already held Ethereum Sepolia ETH
from a PoW faucet, so the pool is filled by depositing through Base's own
L1StandardBridge (scripts/sepolia_to_base_bridge.cljs in nexus-x402).

The bridge address is verified TWO ways, because a deposit to the wrong address
is an ordinary transfer that succeeds and never arrives: against Base's
published contract list, and against the chain, where the OptimismPortal's
systemConfig() must return the SystemConfig Base documents for Sepolia. A
bridge belonging to some other OP-stack chain passes eth_getCode and fails that.

The first deposit REVERTED. A hand-picked 120000 gas limit against a real cost
of 663000: it burnt its limit and arrived nowhere, and `eth_call` on the same
calldata succeeded, so the calldata was never the problem. The script estimates
and adds 25%. Value is refunded on revert; only gas was lost.

Measured: 0.2 ETH at 0xD030410BABA777b1e7FacABf679295641203628A, a key generated
for this and stored as kagi `FAUCET_BASE_SEPOLIA_KEY`. It signs for chain 84532
and the chain id is not a parameter -- a testnet key that can be aimed at
mainnet by a config change is a mainnet key.

## The gate is a Biscuit, and that is not a retreat from the passkey

A passkey is bound to a relying-party id. These credentials are registered to
auth.itonami.cloud, so x402.nexus CANNOT run their ceremony -- the browser
refuses the assertion before any code here would see it. That is WebAuthn, not
a gap in the Worker, and the way past it is not a second rp-id and a second
enrolment.

So the ceremony stays where it is and the faucet consumes what a passkey
sign-in produces: a Biscuit, which this Worker already verified against a
public root it holds. One ceremony implementation, which is what nexus.faucet
asked for in its own docstring.

What that proves is stated rather than implied: the holder held a live
credential from that issuer. NOT that a gesture happened just now -- a Biscuit
outlives the assertion that caused it -- and not a device count, because it
carries no authenticator counter. sign-count is reported as 0 rather than a
fabricated rising number, which would make decide's clone check silently
vacuous. Accounting is per HOLDER: stricter than per credential, not looser.

## The halves are reported separately, always

Gas and USDC arrive by different routes, so nexus.pour decides them
independently. Gas funded and USDC empty is a WORKING faucet and reports as one.
A balance that could not be READ is :balance-unknown and never :pool-exhausted --
those call for different repairs, and reporting the second as the first sends
someone to a funding page instead of a status page. An unreadable draw history
refuses rather than defaulting to `no draws yet`, which would unenforce every
ceiling exactly when the store is failing.

## The sellers had to change, not just be listed

A listing a seller cannot honour is worse than no listing. murakumo priced and
verified on `base` from a private constant, and its vendored pay.x402 fell back
to mainnet USD Coin whatever the network said -- so a base-sepolia offer would
have named a token that does not exist where it told the buyer to send it.

Three things move together: the offer fans out one entry per network (the
facilitator derives exactly one rule per resource and reads its chain from
price.network, so a resource listed once is listed on one chain); the token
comes from a per-network table that REFUSES an unknown network rather than
defaulting; and the chain is read from the payment and checked against an
offered set BEFORE anything uses it. A `(or network chain)` fallback would have
accepted a testnet transaction against a mainnet invoice.

The chain is threaded into the KV cache key and the spend-gate payment-id too.
Tx hashes are independent across chains, so a cache keyed on the hash alone
would let a base-sepolia receipt answer a base payment, and a payment-id without
the chain would let the two share one reservation. Both become reachable the
moment a testnet listing exists.

## Current position, measured 2026-09-01

- x402.nexus: faucet live. GET /faucet reports the pool as measured; POST /faucet
  refuses an absent credential and a malformed one by DIFFERENT names. Gas
  funded, USDC UNFUNDED.
- murakumo.cloud: 8 resources, base + base-sepolia, correct per-network asset and
  per-network EIP-712 domain (USD Coin vs USDC). Listed in the catalog.
- itonami.cloud: covered at hanmoto.itonami.cloud, which listed base-sepolia
  before this work.
- kotobase.net: change written, not yet landed.
- kotoba.cloud: NOT DONE and not merely unfinished. It is a control plane (PQ key
  lifecycle, library publishing) and sells nothing over x402 today, so listing it
  on a testnet means first deciding what it sells and at what price. That is a
  product decision, not a testnet extension, and inventing one would be the
  failure this workspace calls advertising a dish the kitchen cannot cook.

## The loop was open, and that was the real instability (2026-09-01, later)

Sizing was not the whole answer. Measured after the faucet went live: the family
payTo 0xA00366...D4E, which EVERY base-sepolia listing named, is a Safe on
Ethereum mainnet and has NO CONTRACT on Base Sepolia -- code 0, nonce 0 -- and a
Safe address has no private key behind it. Testnet payments sent there were not
misrouted; they were destroyed on arrival.

So the rail burned 100% of what the faucet handed out, against a refill of
10 USDC/24h. No per-draw size and no top-up schedule could make that converge.
The faucet was not small, it was leaking.

All three sellers now route base-sepolia to the faucet pool itself, so what the
faucet pours comes back when it is spent:

  murakumo   MURAKUMO_TREASURY_ADDR_TESTNET
  kotobase   KOTOBASE_TREASURY_ADDR_TESTNET
  hanmoto    TREASURY_ADDR_TESTNET

Mainnet payTo is untouched and prices are unchanged -- the testnet is a
rehearsal of the same claim, not a discount.

Each fails CLOSED: with no testnet address configured the seller stops offering
base-sepolia rather than falling back to the Safe. That fallback is not the
smaller mistake; it is the one that destroys the payment, and the offer still
looks valid from every angle a buyer can see. All three have a test that goes
red when the fallback is reintroduced.

Verified live: all 9 base-sepolia listings across the 3 sellers now name the
pool, in the documents AND in the runtime 402 challenges.

## It was never once bought, and that is how the last bugs were found

Measured at closing: `/admin/settlements` reported ZERO settlements for every
seller, on every chain, ever. The facilitator had been advertising a catalogue
for months and no purchase had completed through it. Nothing said so, because
nothing asked.

Buying one thing found three defects, each of which passed every check that did
not involve money changing hands:

  1. murakumo verified against the WRONG CHAIN. `rpcs` ignored the chain
     entirely and always returned Base mainnet endpoints, so a base-sepolia
     payment was checked where its transaction does not exist. A buyer with
     five confirmations was told `verification_unavailable`. The offer named
     the right chain, token and payTo; only the endpoint list was wrong, and
     nothing that reads an offer can see that.

  2. The GATEWAY settled testnet payments against the mainnet rule.
     `match-rule` returns the first match when a resource is priced on two
     chains, and that is mainnet -- different payTo, different asset.
     `pay.facilitator/rule-for-payment` already existed and does it correctly;
     it simply was not the function the gateway called. This predates the
     testnet work entirely: hanmoto has carried base-sepolia listings alongside
     base all along, so the gateway has never been able to settle a testnet
     payment for a seller that also sells on mainnet.

  3. Re-registering hanmoto through `/apply` DROPPED its origin-token wiring.
     `apply` rebuilds rules from the seller's own document, which cannot carry
     `auth-header` / `auth-secret-env`, so the fields an admin PUT had set were
     replaced with nothing. Self-inflicted, during this work, and restored by
     re-PUTting all six rules.

The first successful purchase: 0.001 USDC on base-sepolia -> HTTP 200 with
hanmoto's real publisher counts. The pool went 20.000 -> 19.912 while the buyer
kept 0.088, which is the recycling working: everything spent came back.

## The published prose was the last surface still lying

llms.txt -- the file an agent reads before deciding how to pay -- still said the
gate was a WebAuthn assertion, verification unwired and the pool unfunded, hours
after all three stopped being true. The page and the JSON had moved to
`nexus.faucet/status`; three hardcoded sentences had not. A description that
UNDERSTATES is still wrong: an agent reading it was told not to bother.

`nexus.directory/faucet-line` now derives one sentence from the measured status,
and a pool that cannot be read says `unmeasured` rather than guessing. murakumo
and kotobase llms.txt now state the two fields that differ between the chains
and point at the faucet.

## What is still owed

The pool is funded: 20 USDC and 0.198 ETH, ~199 draws. Two of the three
draws Circle's faucet issued landed on the wrong chain first (Arc Testnet and
Ethereum Sepolia are the first two entries in its dropdown; Base Sepolia is
seventh), and the Ethereum Sepolia one was recovered with CCTP burn-and-mint --
`nexus.cctp` and `scripts/faucet_topup.cljs` are the programmatic paths for
both problems now. 20 USDC remains on Arc, safe and unused.

Still open:

  - murakumo's payment now VERIFIES and then fails at `reservation_unavailable`.
    Its spend-gate calls kotobase.net/api/payment-reserve; the endpoint is alive
    (401 unauthenticated, correctly) and KOTOBASE_BEARER_TOKEN is set, so it is
    a token-scope or database problem in that subsystem. It is shared with
    mainnet, so it is not testnet-specific -- but that it predates this work is
    UNPROVEN, not established.

  - `/admin/settlements/hanmoto` still reports zero after a successful purchase.
    The settlement index is not recording. An observability gap rather than a
    payment failure, but it means the facilitator's books stay blank while money
    moves.

  - kotoba.cloud sells nothing over x402 and so has no testnet rail to document.
    Listing it means first deciding what it sells and at what price, which is a
    product decision and not this one.")))
